1. Introduction
Open-source software engineering is the process of developing and maintaining software whose source code is publicly available for people to study, use, modify, and improve. Developers and users work together through platforms such as GitHub. They can report problems, suggest improvements, correct errors, and contribute documentation.

2. Problem Analysis
In this case study, a group of developers maintains an open-source Library Management System on GitHub. A student notices that the project does not contain clear instructions explaining how users can issue and return books. Without proper documentation, new users may find the system difficult to understand and use. The student can solve this problem by contributing a documentation file that explains the book issue and return procedures.

3. Proposed Solution
The student can improve the project documentation by following the GitHub contribution workflow. The student first creates a fork of the original repository, makes the required documentation changes in the fork, commits the changes, and submits a Pull Request (PR) to the original project. The project maintainer reviews the contribution and merges it if it meets the project requirements.

4. Steps Involved in the GitHub Contribution Workflow
1. Fork the repository: Open the Library Management System repository on GitHub and click Fork. This creates a personal copy of the project under the student’s GitHub account.
2. Clone the fork (if working locally): Copy the fork’s URL and use the Git clone command to download the project to the computer. This step is useful when editing files with a local editor.
3. Create or edit the documentation file: Open the project documentation folder and create or update a Markdown file such as Book_Issue_Return_Guide.md. Explain the steps for searching for a book, checking its availability, issuing it to a member, recording the due date, returning the book, and confirming that the book has been returned.
4. Commit the changes: Save the updated file and commit it with a meaningful message, for example: “Added book issue and return documentation”. A commit records the changes in the student’s fork.
5. Push changes to GitHub: If the work was completed locally, push the commit to the student’s fork. If the file was edited directly on GitHub, the commit is already saved in the fork.
6. Create a Pull Request: From the student’s fork, choose the option to contribute or open a Pull Request. Set the original repository as the base repository and the student’s fork as the head repository. Add a clear title and explain what documentation was added.
7. Review and merge: The maintainers review the proposed changes and may request corrections. After approval, they merge the Pull Request into the original repository. The documentation then becomes part of the main project.
5. Suggested Documentation Content
The student’s guide can contain the following sections:

• Book search: Enter the book title, author, or ISBN to find a book in the catalogue.
• Availability check: Confirm that the required book is available for issue.
• Book issue: Select the member, choose the available book, and record the issue date and due date.
• Book return: Locate the issued-book record, record the return date, and update the book status.
• Confirmation: Verify that the book is marked as available again and that the member’s record is updated.
